\story[name=main]{
  \dropcap[lines=3]
  From the tapestries that hang in her studio to the drawers of swatches that fill a whole wall,
  Meera Anand's working life has been an argument about thread. For four decades the designer has
  insisted that a garment is only as good as the cloth it is cut from, and that the cloth is only as
  good as the hands that made it.

  Her newest collection, shown last month at the close of the couture week, returned to the idea she
  began with: that the oldest techniques in the country are also its most modern. Beneath long
  coats and fluid skirts sat embroidery that would take a single artisan most of a season to
  finish.

  "I keep coming back to the same few things," she says, turning a length of silk between her
  fingers. "Hand-block prints, the gold thread from the old workshops, the weaves that only three
  or four villages still know. Everything else changes around them."

  \figure[src=/spread/model.png, alt="A model in a black dress with gold cuffs", cols=5-8, cols@wide=7-10, max-width=480px,
          cols@tablet=3-6, cols@phone=1-4, shape=alpha, wrap=contour, wrap-side=both, wrap-offset=10px,
          wrap@phone=jump]

  **How did you first come to textiles?**

  Almost by accident. I studied history, and the part that caught me was the part nobody else
  wanted: the trade records, the lists of cloth that went out through the ports. Then I went to the
  museum stores and saw the actual pieces, and I could not believe how little anyone knew about the
  people who made them.

  **You started with one workshop.**

  Two printers and a borrowed room. We printed on the floor because we had no tables. I sold the
  first scarves from a suitcase, and the first real order came from a shop that wanted forty of a
  design I had made only twice. We learned to repeat ourselves very quickly.

  **What has changed most about the people who buy your clothes?**

  They are younger, and they ask better questions. Ten years ago a customer wanted to know the price.
  Now she wants to know who embroidered the sleeve and how long it took, and whether the dye came
  from a plant or a factory. That makes me very happy, because the answers are the whole point.

  \pullquote[cols=3-6, cols@wide=4-7, max-width=480px, cols@tablet=1-4, wrap=rect, wrap-side=both, hide@phone, style=pq]{
    \span[style=qmark]{“}

    If there is such a thing as quiet luxury here, it was stitched by hand long before anyone gave it
    a name.

    \span[style=qmark]{”}
  }

  **Is it harder now to find the artisans?**

  Harder to keep them. The skills are still there, but a young man in a weaving village can earn
  more driving a taxi in the city. So we pay for the time, not the piece, and we put the maker's
  name on the label. It sounds small. It changes how a family thinks about the work.

  **Your new book is about the workshops, not the clothes.**

  The clothes are the last step. The book follows six crafts from the raw fibre to the finished
  cloth, and most of the photographs are of hands. I wanted people to see the waiting, the drying,
  the mistakes that get unpicked. Nobody photographs the unpicking.

  \figure[src=/spread/textile.jpg, alt="A hand-embroidered panel in blue and ochre thread", cols=10-12, cols@wide=15-16, max-width=340px,
          cols@tablet=5-8, cols@phone=all, ratio=3:4, wrap=rect]{
    \caption{A hand-embroidered panel from the studio archive.}
    \credit{Photo: test image}
  }

  **Does anyone still need a designer, when the craft is the star?**

  Somebody has to hold the thread between the village and the shop. That is the job. I choose what
  to make, and I am stubborn about how, but the beauty was always theirs. I only arrange it.

  **What would you tell someone starting now?**

  Go to the workshops before you draw anything. Sit there for a week. Learn what a hand can do in an
  hour and what it cannot do at all, and then design for the hand, not for the photograph.

  \figure[src=/spread/hands.jpg, alt="Hands tying the fringe of a woven rug", ratio=4:3, wrap=jump]{
    \caption{Finishing a rug by hand: the last step is the fringe.}
    \credit{Photo: test image}
  }

  She has no plans to stop. The studio is already sampling next winter's collection, built around a
  single weave from the eastern hills that, she says, "almost disappeared while nobody was looking."
  The swatches are pinned to the wall by the window, where the light is best, next to a photograph of
  the woman who taught her team to make it.
}

